      "why_read": "Read this to understand how compiling libgit2 to WebAssembly enables high-performance, pure Go repository access without relying on cgo.",
      "hn_id": "50077487",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50077487",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Using Git inside Go applications has historically forced teams to choose between two bad options: go-git, which is pure Go but notoriously slow, or git2go, which wraps libgit2 but requires CGO and breaks seamless cross-compilation.\n\nA new library named go-git2 solves this dilemma through an unconventional pipeline. It compiles the battle-tested C code of libgit2 into WebAssembly, and then translates that WebAssembly directly into Go source code using wasm2go.\n\nBecause the output is genuine Go code, your binaries compile with CGO_ENABLED=0 while running read operations 10 to 100 times faster than go-git. In real-world benchmarks, repository traversals and tree inspections match the speed of the native git command-line tool itself.\n\nWhile it currently handles only read operations, the architectural concept proves that compiling native libraries through WebAssembly to pure Go is a practical alternative to painful foreign function interfaces.\n\nWASM to Go translation may quietly redefine how Go backends consume legacy C libraries."
    },
    {
