      "date": "2026-10-13",
      "authors": [
        "SineFrame"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how clean tool and API design enables lightweight models to match frontier model performance. You will learn how ergonomic server design eliminates common agent failure modes.",
      "hn_id": "50073848",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50073848",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most developers assume that when an AI agent fails a complex task, the model itself is not smart enough. New empirical benchmarks comparing tool interfaces paint a very different picture.\n\nWhen tested on a poorly designed server with seventy raw REST endpoints, Claude Haiku solved only 64 percent of tasks while Claude Opus solved 99 percent. However, when tested on a well-designed server, Haiku solved 99 percent of runs, matching Opus almost run for run across more than one hundred trials.\n\nThe difference came down to basic interface hygiene. The clean server consolidated endpoints into nine focused tools, used flat arguments with explicit enums, returned cursor-based pagination with total counts, and emitted actionable error messages explaining exactly how to repair failed calls.\n\nBefore spending ten times more on larger models for agent harnesses, audit the tool interfaces first. High-context interfaces compensate for brittle architectures, but clean interface design lets smaller, cheaper models perform just as well."
    },
    {
