    {
      "title": "Development at the speed of light with SpacetimeDB",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/ClockworkLabs/SpacetimeDB",
      "date": "2026-10-13",
      "authors": [
        "Clockwork Labs"
      ],
      "why_read": "Explore how SpacetimeDB combines database functionality with application execution to simplify real-time backend infrastructure.",
      "hn_id": "50080139",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50080139",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Traditional backend architectures spend an enormous amount of compute and developer time serializing state across boundaries. You maintain a relational database, an object-relational mapper, an application server, and client-side cache stores, repeatedly translating tables into JSON and back again over network sockets.\n\nSpacetimeDB challenges this paradigm by embedding application logic directly inside the database engine. Instead of treating the database as passive storage queried by external microservices, you write your server domain logic in Rust or TypeScript and compile it to WebAssembly modules that execute inside the database process itself.\n\nTransactions execute directly against in-memory relational tables without intermediate network hops or serialization overhead. When an update commits, the engine computes incremental state diffs and automatically pushes subscription updates to connected clients over WebSockets.\n\nThis design eliminates the need for separate cache layers like Redis or message buses for state synchronization. It reduces typical multi-hop write latencies down to single-digit milliseconds while guaranteeing transactional integrity across all client state mutations.\n\nCollapsing the database and application server into a single execution boundary turns the classic three-tier architecture upside down."
    },
    {
