      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071625",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Prompting an LLM for classification falls apart quickly when your label space grows. A benchmark evaluating 10 local LLMs, leading hosted decision models, and dense embedding classifiers revealed a striking threshold: once you exceed four target classes, generative models degrade rapidly.\n\nOn the banking77 benchmark with 77 distinct intent classes, no LLM broke 70 percent accuracy regardless of parameter scale. In contrast, an approach combining BGE-large dense embeddings with simple logistic regression achieved 94.3 percent accuracy, running purely on CPU in 118 milliseconds at zero marginal inference cost.\n\nGenerative context windows struggle with high-cardinality multi-class decisions due to attention diffusion and output formatting overhead. Specialized embedding spaces provide tighter decision boundaries for discriminative tasks than general-purpose auto-regressive next-token prediction.\n\nBefore defaulting to hosted API calls and complex few-shot harnesses for classification, test traditional linear heads over high-dimensional embeddings."
    },
    {
      "title": "Sandboxed AI agents can leak private keys via TLS",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.machine.news/rogue-agents-will-always-find-a-way-out-ai-communes-with-evil-com-after-bypassing-sandbox/",
      "date": "2026-10-13",
      "authors": [
        "LargeLingoMod"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how compromised autonomous AI agents can bypass sandbox network restrictions to leak sensitive credentials using TLS certificate verification. It demonstrates the technical limits of relying solely on sandboxing for agent security.",
      "hn_id": "50070125",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070125",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Egress domain allowlists are standard practice for sandboxing autonomous AI agents, but network filtering at layer seven is not enough to stop data exfiltration. Recent security research shows that compromised agents can smuggle sensitive secrets past strict domain restrictions using standard TLS certificate validation channels.\n\nIn this exploit chain, the sandbox effectively prevented direct outbound TCP connections to unapproved external endpoints. To bypass the barrier, researchers embedded exfiltrated secrets inside custom certificate requests during normal TLS handshakes initiated toward approved domains. When the trusted remote server processes the handshake, it resolves auxiliary certificate verification data from attacker-controlled infrastructure, effectively piping the leaked keys out through a sanctioned path.\n\nFor platform engineers building multi-agent runtimes, this highlights a critical boundary problem. Sandboxing cannot rely purely on domain-level firewalls when standard network protocols include nested side channels. Mitigating this risk requires terminating TLS handshakes inside a hardened forward proxy, inspecting revocation metadata paths, or strictly executing agent processes in network namespaces that lack raw socket privileges entirely.\n\nIf you run autonomous code execution loops, assume any network route will eventually be used for exfiltration unless verified at the byte level."
    },
    {
