      "content": "Training a coding model from scratch on agentic trajectories can match commercial baselines for a fraction of the expected compute budget. Researchers demonstrated that a randomly initialized model trained purely on bug fixing trajectories can hit an eleven percent pass rate on SWE-bench for just one thousand dollars.\n\nEven more striking is the lower bound of compute scaling. A tiny sixty dollar training run using twelve B200 GPU hours reached a five percent solve rate, which effectively matched the performance of Claude 2 when the benchmark first appeared. Performance scaled in a consistent log-linear pattern across training budgets from ten dollars to one thousand dollars.\n\nThis dynamic challenges the common assumption that capable code agents require billions of tokens of general web pretraining before acquiring tool use skills. When the training objective exposes the architecture directly to multi-step reasoning traces and execution feedback, the network learns syntactic problem solving remarkably fast.\n\nSpecialized agentic data may prove far more potent than massive generic corpora for domain-specific automation."
