      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071427",
      "image": null,
      "content": "High-throughput hashing algorithms often face a sharp trade-off between bulk streaming throughput and short-key latency. SiliciumHash manages to beat standard baselines like xxHash3 and wyhash on both fronts by combining AVX2 SIMD instructions with SIMD-within-a-register (SWAR) techniques.\n\nIn microbenchmarks on Zen 3 architectures, the algorithm achieves bulk streaming throughput exceeding 103 gigabytes per second, fully saturating the memory roofline and delivering a 66 percent improvement over xxHash3. For 16-byte keys, latency drops to roughly 0.41 nanoseconds, effectively doubling small-key lookup throughput. It has also passed all quality, avalanche, and collision tests inside SMHasher, leading to its upstream merge.\n\nFor engineers designing query execution engines or in-memory SwissTable hash joins, hash calculation often becomes a bottleneck during heavy aggregation. Integrating an associative table engine with tailored SIMD hashing can yield substantial speedups on cache hits without sacrificing distribution uniformity.\n\nHardware-conscious algorithm design still yields massive performance wins even against battle-tested industry standards."
